ly with standard matmul?\n\n# If q is (B, L, H*D), k is (B, L, H*D)...\n# No, that doesn't work because we need per-head computation.\n\n# What if we think of it as:\n# q shape (B*H, L, D), k shape (B*H, L, D)\n# q = xr @ wq.T  # (B*L, H*D)\n# We want (B*H, L, D)\n# This is a reshape + transpose: (B*L, H, D) -> (B, L, H, D) -> (B, H, L, D) -> (B*H, L, D)\n# The issue is that L and H are swapped, so it's not a simple reshape.\n\n# Let me check if there's a smarter way...\n# What if we layout weights as (D, H, C) -> no, same problem.\n\n# Actually, let me time the reshape/permute/contiguous overhead\nq_flat = xr @ wq.reshape(HD,C).T\ntimeit('reshape+permute+contig q', lambda: q_flat.reshape(B,L,H,D).permute(0,2,1,3).contiguous())\n\n# What if we don't make contiguous and let the matmul handle it?\nq_nc = q_flat.reshape(B,L,H,D).permute(0,2,1,3)  # not contiguous\nk_nc = (xr @ wk.reshape(HD,C).T).reshape(B,L,H,D).permute(0,2,1,3)\ntimeit('logits non-contig', lambda: q_nc @ k_nc.transpose(-1,-2))\n\nq_c = q_nc.contiguous()\nk_c = k_nc.contiguous()\ntimeit('logits contig', lambda: q_c @ k_c.transpose(-1,-2))\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"3 separate: 42.484 ms\nfused 3: 46.135 ms\nfused q+k + v sep: 42.668 ms\nF.linear q: 14.020 ms\nreshape+permute+contig q: 0.066 ms\nlogits non-contig: 4.178 ms\nlogits contig: 2.844 ms\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch, time, math\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C)\nwq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n\nHD = H * D\nxr = x.reshape(B*L, C)\n\ndef timeit(name, fn, n=7):\n    times = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        t0 = time.process_time_ns()\n        fn()\n        times.append((time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 1e6)\n    print(f'{name}: {sorted(times)[len(times)//2]:.3f} ms')\n\n# Now let's think about the p1@G and pmix@G optimization more carefully.\n# Current: compute p1@G and pmix@G separately (2 matmuls)\n# Idea: since pmix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1, and we already have p0, p1\n# pmix@G = (1-beta)*p0@G + beta*p1@G\n# So we need p0@G and p1@G (2 matmuls), then combine\n# Same number of matmuls, no win.\n\n# But what if we only need norms?\n# ||m||\"'^2 = sum_c (sum_j pmix[b,h,i,j] * x[b,j,c])''^2\n# = sum_j sum_k pmix[b,h,i,j] * pmix[b,h,i,k] * sum_c x[b,j,c]*x[b,k,c]\n# = pmix @ G @ pmix''^T ... but only diagonal elements\n# i.e. normm_sq[b,h,i] = (pmix[b,h,i,:] @ G[b,:,:] @ pmix[b,h,i,:].T)\n# which is exactly what we have: (pmix @ G) * pmix summed over last dim\n\n# Can we compute both norms from a single factorization?\n# If G = U S U''^T (eigen-decomposition), then norm_sq = ||pmix @ U @ sqrt(S)||''^2\n# But G is LxL symmetric, eigendecomp is O(L''^3)... not worth it for L=256.\n\n# What about using Cholesky? G = L L''^T, then norm = ||pmix @ L||_F\n# Still O(L''^3) for Cholesky of LxL.\n# Actually Cholesky of 256x256 is fast... let'\"'s check.\n\nG = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)  # (B, L, L)\nprint('G shape:', G.shape)\n\ntimeit('cholesky G', lambda: torch.linalg.cholesky(G))\n# Then norm1 = ||p1 @ L||_F per head/token... still needs p1 @ L which is p1 @ L (B,H,L,L)\n# Same cost as p1 @ G.\n\n# What about computing the norms as we go? \n# p0 = softmax(logits), p1 = softmax(perturbed)\n# p0@x has shape (B,H,L,C), norm = sqrt(sum_c ...)\n# Current Gram approach: O(B*L\"'^2*C + 2*B*H*L''^2)\n# Direct: O(2*B*H*L*C) for computing two norms via p@x... no wait.\n\n# Actually let me reconsider. The output is p_mix @ v where v = x @ wv''^T.\n# v has shape (B,H,L,D) with D=64, so p_mix @ v is O(B*H*L''^2*D) = 20*256*256*64 = 84M\n# That'\"'s the output computation.\n\n# For norms we need ||u1|| = ||p1 @ x|| and ||m|| = ||p_mix @ x|| (in C space)\n# Gram approach cost:\n#   G = x @ x\"'^T: B * L * L * C = 256*256*1280 = 84M\n#   p1 @ G: B*H*L*L = 20*256*256 = 1.3M ... wait no, matmul cost is B*H*L*L*L = 20*256''^3 = 336M\n#   pmix @ G: B*H*L*L*L = 336M\n# Total: 84 + 336 + 336 = 756M FLOPs\n\n# Direct approach (p @ x then norm):\n#   p1 @ x: B*H*L*L*C = 20*256*256*1280 = 1.68B\n#   norm: B*H*L*C = negligible\n#   pmix @ x: 1.68B\n# Total: 3.36B -- much worse\n\n# Gram is way better. What if we just compute one matmul differently?\n# p0@G is also B*H*L*L*L... but we don'\"'t need p0@G directly.\n\n# Wait, we can derive ||m|| from ||u0||, ||u1||, and cross term:\n# m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0) = (1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1\n# ||m||\"'^2 = (1-beta)''^2 * ||u0||''^2 + beta''^2 * ||u1||''^2 + 2*(1-beta)*beta * u0 . u1\n# u0 . u1 = sum_c (p0@x)[c] * (p1@x)[c] = sum_c sum_j sum_k p0[j] x[j,c] p1[k] x[k,c]\n#          = sum_j sum_k p0[j] p1[k] sum_c x[j,c]*x[k,c] = p0 @ G @ p1''^T (just for the matching i)\n# i.e. per (b,h,i): sum_j sum_k p0[b,h,i,j] * p1[b,h,i,k] * G[b,j,k]\n# = (p0[b,h,i,:] @ G[b,:,:]) @ p1[b,h,i,:].T\n# = (p0 @ G) * p1 summed over last dim\n\n# So we need:\n# - ||u0||''^2: (p0 @ G) * p0 summed -> needs p0 @ G\n# - ||u1||''^2: (p1 @ G) * p1 summed -> needs p1 @ G  \n# - cross: (p0 @ G) * p1 summed -> needs p0 @ G\n# Total: 2 matmuls (p0@G and p1@G), then all norms/cross are cheap elementwise\n\n# Current: p1@G and pmix@G = 2 matmuls\n# Proposed: p0@G and p1@G = 2 matmuls (same cost, maybe pmix is slightly cheaper due to sparsity?)\n\n# Let me verify: is p_mix @ G meaningfully different in cost from p0 @ G?\n# They'\"'re the same shape and density. So same cost.\n\n# The real question: can we save 1 matmul?\n# We always need at least p1@G (for ||u1||). And we need ||m||.\n# If we use p0@G + p1@G, we can compute both ||u0||, ||u1||, and all cross terms.\n# Then ||m|| from formula. Same 2 matmuls.\n\n# What if we compute p0@G, and then derive p1@G from p0@G? \n# No, p0 and p1 are different softmaxes of different logits.\n\n# Hmm. Let me think about another angle:\n# Can we avoid computing G by using x directly in a smarter way?\n\n# What if we think of it as:\n# For each head h, we need:\n#   - p_h @ v_h (output)\n#   - ||p_h @ x|| (norms for ratio)\n# \n# Could we compute x @ something small and use that?\n# If we do a low-rank approximation of the norm... no, must be exact.\n\n# OK let me try another angle: what about the softmax?\n# Can we compute p0 and p1 more efficiently?\n# p0 = softmax(A)\n# p1 = softmax(a_p * A + a_m * A\"'^T)\n# Both are softmaxes of LxL matrices.\n\n# Actually, wait. Let me look at the total again:\n# Projections: 45ms\n# q@k''^T: 4.7ms\n# softmax p0: 14ms\n# p1_logits: 8.7ms (or 3.7 with in-place)\n# softmax p1: 13ms\n# p_mix: 0.5ms\n# Gram: 3.6ms\n# p1@G: 17ms\n# pmix@G: 12ms\n# p_mix@v: 3ms\n# Total: ~125ms\n\n# The p@G matmuls are huge (17+12=29ms). Why is p1@G 17ms but pmix@G only 12ms?\n# Maybe due to memory layout or just noise. Let me check.\n\np0 = torch.randn(B,H,L,L).softmax(dim=-1)\np1 = torch.randn(B,H,L,L).softmax(dim=-1)\npmix = 0.5*p0 + 0.5*p1\nG = x @ x.transpose(-1,-2).unsqueeze(1)  # B,1,L,L... no wait\nG = x @ x.transpose(-1,-2)  # B,L,L\nG_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)  # B,1,L,L\n\ntimeit('\"'p0@G', lambda: p0 @ G_exp)\ntimeit('p1@G', lambda: p1 @ G_exp)\ntimeit('pmix@G', lambda: pmix @ G_exp)\n# The difference is probably noise. Let me run more iters.\n\n# Actually I bet the issue is memory layout.\n# p0 from softmax is contiguous (output is always contiguous)\n# pmix from lerp might be... let me check\nprint('p0 contig:', p0.is_contiguous())\nprint('pmix contig:', pmix.is_contiguous())\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
